+    "title": "Continuity key ledger: reserve, release, bind and conflict records",
+    "description": "Spec §4.2. owns: store::continuity_keys, the service dispatch of ReserveContinuityKey and ReleaseContinuityKey. consumes: schema delta 16.\nblocked-by ht-ma6i.5: consumes boundary contract\nboundary contract: ht-ma6i.5\nLands the store ledger and the service operations over it: stateless functions over the caller's transaction, plus Reserve and Release wired through service_controls::operate. It judges nothing (KeyVerdict does) and touches no seat.\nfiles: src/store/continuity_keys.rs (new), src/store/mod.rs, src/store/service_controls.rs, TRUST-POLICY.md\nAcceptance:\n- [ ] Reserve is idempotent for the owner and returns the bound seat; another author is refused; release works for an unbound key and is refused for a bound one.\n- [ ] Bind enforces one key per seat and one seat per key; lookup by seat and by key.\n- [ ] Reserve with a seat binds at once, recording bound_by service_reservation (check-in binding records check_in), accepted only for a local, resolved or unresolved, unnamed Herdr seat holding no other key with the key unbound or already bound to it; replay and re-reserve to the same seat are no-ops; a key bound elsewhere or a seat holding another key is Conflict; a retired, named or hc seat is refused; nothing moves. TRUST-POLICY defines both bound_by values and the accepted limit.\n- [ ] Recording a conflict writes one conflict row and one alert in one write; a repeat writes neither; the conflict table holds its 256-row cap (a full table records nothing and reports it to the caller).\n- [ ] Release-on-final removes the key and its conflict rows; conflict rows can be cleared by key and by (host, target).\n- [ ] ReserveContinuityKey (with and without a seat) and ReleaseContinuityKey work over a real service_session_v3 connection: owner authorization, operation-key replay and payload-mismatch refusal are tested through the session, not only the store. An already-running clone's resolved seat gains a key this way.\n- [ ] The alert log evicts its oldest row at the cap with ordinals still increasing: the 257th conflict still alerts.\n- [ ] A re-judged conflict on an existing (host, target) row updates key, kind, seat and owner, keeping first_seen_at; a new alert is appended only when the row is new or its key or kind changed; an unchanged refusal writes nothing. An alert-only kind never holds, and its row clears when the owner it was judged against stops being the pane's resolved owner.\n- [ ] A row's alert is appended when the row is new or changed, as the spec's Key abstractions states.",
