+    "title": "Graph clone identity: threads side (herdr-graph H2 design)",
+    "description": "Threads side of herdr-graph's clone\u003c-\u003eseat identity design (/var/home/alepar/AleCode/herdr-graph/docs/reviews/2026-10-29-seat-review-H2-identity-design.md, approved by the user in the herdr-graph seat-review session 2026-11-04, relayed by snSGS7Z5UIwq). G1 (ship ht-hsyg) is done: v2.8.2 2026-11-01, current v3.0.0. Children: G2, D1, D2, G5. Herdr unchanged; no service ever places a seat.\n\nSpec: docs/superpowers/runs/2026-11-04-graph-clone-identity/2026-11-04-graph-clone-identity-design.md\n\n## Global Constraints\nRun rules: work only on per-task worktrees under this run; never commit to main, never git push, never git stash. Run cargo through /var/home/alepar/.cache/herdr-threads-heavy/heavy /var/home/alepar/.cache/herdr-threads-heavy/tb \u003ccmd\u003e (the host has no gcc). Never use the real Herdr server or socket, herdr plugin link/unlink, real setup or install.sh, or real user config (~/.claude, ~/.codex, ~/.aisw, ~/.gemini); tests use isolated Herdr sessions (scripts/lib/isolated-herdr.sh). No build output in /tmp. Herdr-threads writes (send, ack, accept) are forbidden to subagents. Every commit carries the Outside-adapter trailer and Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e. Per-change checks: clippy (--all-targets --all-features -D warnings), scripts/check-harness-neutral, scripts/check-wire-compat. TRUST-POLICY.md is normative and updated in the same commit as a change that adds provenance, an invariant or an accepted limit. Model-visible strings follow AGENTS.md token rules. Tests create temp paths only through test_support::scratch::temp_path and spawn children through test_support::spawn.\n\nA task worktree starts from a warm build: run `scripts/seed-worktree-target \u003cintegration worktree\u003e/target \u003ctask worktree\u003e` first (copy-on-write; never build from scratch). Run tests focused on the change (`heavy tb cargo test --locked --all-features \u003cfilter\u003e`); never the full suite (the caller runs it once). The heavy wrapper holds one of two shared slots, so never run heavy jobs in parallel from one task. Check `df -h` before a large write. Stop every process a test starts.\n\n\u003c!-- key-abstractions digest: begin --\u003e\n- `store::continuity_keys`: owns key reservation, binding, conflicts and alerts with their caps and clearing rules; new key bookkeeping goes here;\n  callers never write these tables or judge outcomes.\n- `KeyVerdict`: owns the §4.2 outcome table as one pure judge; a new outcome or conflict kind is one variant and one arm here;\n  callers gather facts and act, and never restate the table.\n- `store::seats::reversible`: owns the reversible, unretired and final transitions, the deadline shift and the frozen and waiting SQL fragments; new lifecycle rules go here;\n  callers never restate those predicates, and it never places a seat or settles obligations.\n- `RetirementInputs` and `RetirementStart`: own what a retirement takes and whether it yielded a job; new evidence goes in the inputs;\n  callers never update `retirements` after the call or assume a job exists.\n- `identity::shell_continuity`: owns the pure G2 judgment over `ProcessInfo`, built on `process_continuity::judge`; new shell facts go here;\n  callers never compare fingerprints themselves, and it never touches the store or Herdr.\n- `ServiceSessionLevel`: owns which session version each service operation requires; a new operation is one arm in its exhaustive match;\n  callers never test session version flags or list operations.\n\u003c!-- key-abstractions digest: end --\u003e",
